WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.683
Welcome to Module 1 of the
learning path Defender X D R

2
00:00:03.683 --> 00:00:08.225
Engineering and Operations:
architecture, access and onboarding.

3
00:00:08.225 --> 00:00:12.175
Before any prevention setting
matters, two things have to be true.

4
00:00:12.175 --> 00:00:16.525
The right people can see and act on
the service, in the tenant's own cloud.

5
00:00:16.525 --> 00:00:19.225
And the sensor is on
every device it should be.

6
00:00:19.225 --> 00:00:23.913
In this module you learn the service's
shape and its G C C limits, you set who can

7
00:00:23.913 --> 00:00:28.825
do what, and you prove the sensor's coverage
with a query whose result you can file.

8
00:00:28.825 --> 00:00:33.963
The module covers Steps 1 and 2 of the
Defender X D R implementation guide.

9
00:00:33.963 --> 00:00:38.112
It takes about forty minutes, plus
Lab 2 in your own test tenant.

10
00:00:38.112 --> 00:00:42.345
The path covers Windows clients
and Windows Server only,

11
00:00:42.345 --> 00:00:46.840
for a civilian agency in
Microsoft three sixty-five G C C.

12
00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:51.628
Why start here?

13
00:00:51.628 --> 00:00:53.427
The guide opens with one idea.

14
00:00:53.427 --> 00:00:56.303
A Defender license is
not a defended endpoint.

15
00:00:56.303 --> 00:00:59.056
The sensor has to be on
every device and healthy,

16
00:00:59.056 --> 00:01:03.080
the prevention settings have to be
enforced and not just configured,

17
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.328
and every alert has to end in
a decision someone can show.

18
00:01:06.328 --> 00:01:09.914
A license gives you the right to
run the sensor and the service.

19
00:01:09.914 --> 00:01:14.400
It doesn't put the sensor on every device,
it doesn't give anyone the right role,

20
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.028
and it doesn't connect the security
operations center to the tenant's own cloud.

21
00:01:19.028 --> 00:01:23.090
So this module proves three things
before anything else is built.

22
00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:24.840
The right people can see and act,

23
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.340
through roles assigned with
privileged identity management.

24
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.465
The S O C reads incidents through
Microsoft Graph, in the tenant's own cloud.

25
00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:37.093
And every in-scope device is
onboarded with an active sensor,

26
00:01:37.093 --> 00:01:39.777
or is in the exception
register with a reason.

27
00:01:39.777 --> 00:01:44.960
Gates 1 and 2 come first in the guide,
and every later gate depends on them.

28
00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.623
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

29
00:01:51.623 --> 00:01:55.224
Name Defender X D R's components,
its two trust boundaries,

30
00:01:55.224 --> 00:01:58.060
and which control plane
holds which setting.

31
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.724
Read the guide's G C C availability table,

32
00:02:00.724 --> 00:02:05.972
and record a feature that isn't available
as a disposition, not a failed control.

33
00:02:05.972 --> 00:02:09.660
Describe the program's unified
role-based access control roles,

34
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.747
and why they're assigned through pim.

35
00:02:11.747 --> 00:02:16.260
Connect to Microsoft Graph in
G C C, and read incidents.

36
00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.698
And prove onboarding and sensor health,
on one device and across the fleet.

37
00:02:20.698 --> 00:02:24.580
Before you start, read the guide's
How to read this guide section and its

38
00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.190
prerequisites, and skim
the Privileged Access

39
00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:30.335
implementation guide's pim
model for the eligible roles.

40
00:02:30.335 --> 00:02:34.060
Lab 2 runs in a test
tenant, never production.

41
00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.122
Unit 1 is the shape of the service.

42
00:02:40.122 --> 00:02:44.402
Defender X D R joins Defender for
Endpoint, Defender for Identity,

43
00:02:44.402 --> 00:02:47.731
Defender for Office three
sixty-five and Defender

44
00:02:47.731 --> 00:02:51.060
for Cloud Apps in one portal
and one incident queue.

45
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.860
Two trust boundaries matter.

46
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.785
The agency's boundary holds the devices,
the S O C's tools and the evidence store.

47
00:02:58.785 --> 00:03:04.400
Microsoft's holds the service, and for a
G C C tenant, as Microsoft Learn's page

48
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.197
on Defender for U S Government describes,
it has its own G C C service endpoints.

49
00:03:10.197 --> 00:03:11.760
Then the control planes.

50
00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.552
Learn says Intune is the recommended tool
for distributing Defender for Endpoint

51
00:03:16.552 --> 00:03:20.025
settings, and that its policies
apply to device objects only,

52
00:03:20.025 --> 00:03:23.498
so they're assigned to Entra
device groups, never user groups.

53
00:03:23.498 --> 00:03:26.959
Settings Intune doesn't
hold, like automation levels

54
00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:30.185
and custom detections, live
in the Defender portal.

55
00:03:30.185 --> 00:03:35.755
Servers in Azure, A W S, Google Cloud or
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure reach Defender

56
00:03:35.755 --> 00:03:38.704
through Defender for
Servers and Azure Arc,

57
00:03:38.704 --> 00:03:41.735
as the Devices and
Endpoints book describes.

58
00:03:41.735 --> 00:03:45.216
And one rule above all: one
control plane per setting,

59
00:03:45.216 --> 00:03:48.880
because Group Policy wins
over Intune by default.

60
00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:57.224
Unit 2 is what G C C has, from
the guide's availability table,

61
00:03:57.224 --> 00:04:01.330
read on Microsoft Learn on
November eleventh, 2026.

62
00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:06.922
Learn's page on Defender for Endpoint for
U S Government customers says prevention,

63
00:04:06.922 --> 00:04:11.217
detection, investigation and
remediation are available in G C C.

64
00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:14.580
The portal is security
dot microsoft dot com.

65
00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.215
Graph's incident and hunting
A P I's are available,

66
00:04:18.215 --> 00:04:20.942
with G C C on the worldwide Graph host.

67
00:04:20.942 --> 00:04:21.918
Then the gap.

68
00:04:21.918 --> 00:04:26.736
Learn's page on Security Exposure
Management says it's available in Public

69
00:04:26.736 --> 00:04:31.167
Cloud only, so attack paths and the
exposure graph aren't there for G C C.

70
00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:35.526
And Learn doesn't say whether custom
detection rules through Graph,

71
00:04:35.526 --> 00:04:40.429
automatic attack disruption, or web
content filtering on Windows reach G C C.

72
00:04:40.429 --> 00:04:44.142
The guide's rule: not yet
specified means Learn doesn't say,

73
00:04:44.142 --> 00:04:45.643
not that it's available.

74
00:04:45.643 --> 00:04:49.548
A feature G C C doesn't offer
goes in the decision record and

75
00:04:49.548 --> 00:04:54.300
the closure register as not
applicable, never as a failed control.

76
00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.000
Unit 3 is who can do what.

77
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.373
Microsoft Learn describes four permission
groups in Defender's unified role-based

78
00:05:05.373 --> 00:05:08.903
access control: security
operations, security posture,

79
00:05:08.903 --> 00:05:12.050
authorization and settings,
and data operations.

80
00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:16.537
The program builds four roles from them;
that's its risk-based recommendation.

81
00:05:16.537 --> 00:05:19.136
A read role, called Security data basics,

82
00:05:19.136 --> 00:05:22.363
for analysts and the
assessor's read-only account.

83
00:05:22.363 --> 00:05:27.025
A responder role that adds alerts,
response and basic live response.

84
00:05:27.025 --> 00:05:30.382
A detection engineer role
with Detection tuning,

85
00:05:30.382 --> 00:05:33.575
manage, which the custom
detection A P I accepts.

86
00:05:33.575 --> 00:05:37.238
And advanced live response
only for Tier zero analysts.

87
00:05:37.238 --> 00:05:41.737
Each role is assigned to groups that pim
governs, so no one holds it standing.

88
00:05:41.737 --> 00:05:46.012
Create every role and assignment
before you activate the workloads;

89
00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:50.587
Learn says activation needs at least
Security Administrator in Entra I D.

90
00:05:50.587 --> 00:05:53.512
Then connect the S O C's read path.

91
00:05:53.512 --> 00:05:57.549
Learn names Security Incident dot
Read dot All as the least-privileged

92
00:05:57.549 --> 00:06:02.063
permission to list incidents, and Threat
Hunting dot Read dot All to run queries.

93
00:06:02.063 --> 00:06:07.624
Connect with the environment of the tenant's
cloud: Global, the value for G C C.

94
00:06:07.624 --> 00:06:10.759
A session in the wrong
cloud returns an empty list,

95
00:06:10.759 --> 00:06:13.250
and an empty list looks like a pass.

96
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.888
Unit 4 is onboarding, by management plane.

97
00:06:19.888 --> 00:06:25.185
Windows clients onboard through Intune's
endpoint detection and response policy,

98
00:06:25.185 --> 00:06:29.176
E S oh-oh-four in the Devices and
Endpoints book's policy library,

99
00:06:29.176 --> 00:06:31.863
with the package type Auto from connector.

100
00:06:31.863 --> 00:06:36.612
Assign it to ring zero, then ring
one, then rings two and three.

101
00:06:36.612 --> 00:06:40.502
Windows Server onboards through
Defender for Servers on Azure Arc.

102
00:06:40.502 --> 00:06:45.503
Microsoft Learn's government page says
Windows Server twenty nineteen needs update K

103
00:06:45.503 --> 00:06:48.767
B four five eight six eight
three nine before onboarding,

104
00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:50.851
so it reaches the right environment.

105
00:06:50.851 --> 00:06:54.863
The program's own standard assumes
twenty nineteen as the minimum.

106
00:06:54.863 --> 00:06:59.638
Rollback is the offboarding package,
deployed as an E D R policy.

107
00:06:59.638 --> 00:07:03.007
Learn says the package expires
seven days after download,

108
00:07:03.007 --> 00:07:07.851
and you never deploy onboarding and offboarding
to the same device at the same time.

109
00:07:07.851 --> 00:07:11.565
A device the sensor can't reach,
such as an unsupported system,

110
00:07:11.565 --> 00:07:13.794
an isolated network or an appliance,

111
00:07:13.794 --> 00:07:17.510
goes in the exception register
with a compensating control:

112
00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:21.671
the streamlined connectivity set or
a proxy, or, where no path exists,

113
00:07:21.671 --> 00:07:25.312
Windows Event Forwarding of
the Defender operational log,

114
00:07:25.312 --> 00:07:29.610
which Learn names for centralizing
events without the service.

115
00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.110
How do you know the sensor is healthy?

116
00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:37.735
On the device, the
sense service is running

117
00:07:37.735 --> 00:07:40.660
and the onboarding state
in the registry is one.

118
00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:44.341
In the service, the device info
table carries two columns:

119
00:07:44.341 --> 00:07:47.160
onboarding status, and
sensor health state.

120
00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:52.423
Microsoft Learn's device inventory field
reference lists five sensor health values.

121
00:07:52.423 --> 00:07:53.873
Active is the pass.

122
00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:58.097
Inactive means no contact,
typically for seven days or more.

123
00:07:58.097 --> 00:08:02.460
Then no sensor data, impaired
communications, and misconfigured.

124
00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.474
Every value but active is a ticket;
for impaired communications,

125
00:08:06.474 --> 00:08:10.260
start with the government U R L
list the firewall has to allow.

126
00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:15.672
Intune's E D R onboarding status report
shows the same thing from the Intune side.

127
00:08:15.672 --> 00:08:19.756
The guide sets the day-two rule: the
operator reviews coverage weekly,

128
00:08:19.756 --> 00:08:23.984
and every device that hasn't been
active for seven days becomes a ticket.

129
00:08:23.984 --> 00:08:26.797
The weekly coverage file
is kept twelve months.

130
00:08:26.797 --> 00:08:30.710
The platform owner reviews the
exception register every quarter.

131
00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:34.235
And the assessor-ready
artifact is coverage by ring,

132
00:08:34.235 --> 00:08:38.120
against the inventory the
C M eight control asks for.

133
00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:47.245
Lab 2 runs the guide's own validate commands
in a test tenant, never production.

134
00:08:47.245 --> 00:08:50.242
The authors haven't run
them, as the page says,

135
00:08:50.242 --> 00:08:54.820
and the path has no Lab 1 on fixtures,
because the guide has no kit yet.

136
00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:59.515
You need a test tenant licensed for Defender
for Endpoint Plan 2, a reader account

137
00:08:59.515 --> 00:09:04.420
activated through pim, and a ring zero
Windows test device onboarded through Intune.

138
00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:07.132
Values you supply are in angle brackets.

139
00:09:07.132 --> 00:09:11.951
Step one, on your workstation: install
the two Graph modules, connect with the

140
00:09:11.951 --> 00:09:16.769
environment for your cloud, and create
a folder for this lab's evidence files.

141
00:09:16.769 --> 00:09:18.657
Step two reads incidents.

142
00:09:18.657 --> 00:09:22.046
As the guide states it, a
pass is up to five incidents,

143
00:09:22.046 --> 00:09:25.757
or an empty list in a new
tenant, with no permission error.

144
00:09:25.757 --> 00:09:30.008
An error naming the role means the
account lacks one of the reader roles.

145
00:09:30.008 --> 00:09:33.593
Step three runs on the test
device, in an elevated session:

146
00:09:33.593 --> 00:09:38.170
query the sense service, and read the
onboarding state from the registry.

147
00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:42.480
Microsoft Learn gives the value
one for an onboarded device.

148
00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.580
Step four files the fleet's coverage.

149
00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.859
The query takes the latest
record for each Windows device

150
00:09:51.859 --> 00:09:55.343
and counts them by onboarding
status and sensor health state.

151
00:09:55.343 --> 00:09:58.954
It runs through Graph so the
result is a file you can keep.

152
00:09:58.954 --> 00:10:03.617
On the slide it's wrapped to fit; on
the page each command is one line.

153
00:10:03.617 --> 00:10:07.604
The guide's pass: every device
onboarded with an active sensor,

154
00:10:07.604 --> 00:10:09.480
or in the exception register.

155
00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.737
Learn doesn't list the exact
onboarding status strings,

156
00:10:12.737 --> 00:10:15.993
so the query groups by them
rather than filtering on one.

157
00:10:15.993 --> 00:10:20.379
Step five is Microsoft's detection
test, on the test device, under its

158
00:10:20.379 --> 00:10:25.392
change record, from an elevated Command
Prompt, exactly as Learn publishes it.

159
00:10:25.392 --> 00:10:28.367
Learn says an alert appears
in about ten minutes.

160
00:10:28.367 --> 00:10:30.393
One caution the guide records:

161
00:10:30.393 --> 00:10:35.903
Learn's page lists Windows Server versions
as supported, not Windows ten or eleven,

162
00:10:35.903 --> 00:10:38.981
so on a client a missing
alert is inconclusive;

163
00:10:38.981 --> 00:10:43.356
rely on the coverage query and
Module 2's network protection test.

164
00:10:43.356 --> 00:10:44.893
Step six backs it out.

165
00:10:44.893 --> 00:10:47.664
Nothing changed but the
alert the test raised:

166
00:10:47.664 --> 00:10:51.450
resolve that incident in the
test tenant, and disconnect.

167
00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.512
The federal requirement, from
the guide's requirements table.

168
00:10:58.512 --> 00:11:03.807
Gate one supports A C two and A C six from
NIST special publication eight hundred

169
00:11:03.807 --> 00:11:08.300
fifty-three, revision five: account
management and least privilege,

170
00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:10.787
here for the security tooling itself.

171
00:11:10.787 --> 00:11:16.225
It also supports A U nine, enhancement
four, which limits access to audit tools.

172
00:11:16.225 --> 00:11:20.025
The evidence is the role export
and the pim assignment report.

173
00:11:20.025 --> 00:11:25.179
Gate two supports C M eight, the system
component inventory, and S I four,

174
00:11:25.179 --> 00:11:30.599
system monitoring, along with O M B
memorandum M twenty-two oh-nine's device

175
00:11:30.599 --> 00:11:35.753
actions, including endpoint detection
and response deployed widely,

176
00:11:35.753 --> 00:11:38.063
as M twenty-two oh-one describes.

177
00:11:38.063 --> 00:11:41.751
The evidence is the coverage
file and the exception register.

178
00:11:41.751 --> 00:11:46.685
Each row lines up with a fed-ramp twenty
X key security indicator, which binds

179
00:11:46.685 --> 00:11:52.012
cloud service providers; the agency uses
them as a vocabulary for its own evidence.

180
00:11:52.012 --> 00:11:54.050
The gates support these controls.

181
00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:57.420
The assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

182
00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:03.145
Check yourself with two questions.

183
00:12:03.145 --> 00:12:08.457
First: an Attack Surface Reduction policy
in Intune is assigned to a user group.

184
00:12:08.457 --> 00:12:09.470
What happens?

185
00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:10.695
The answer is b.

186
00:12:10.695 --> 00:12:14.081
It never applies, because
Microsoft Learn says Defender

187
00:12:14.081 --> 00:12:17.395
for Endpoint management
supports device objects only.

188
00:12:17.395 --> 00:12:19.495
Assign it to an Entra device group.

189
00:12:19.495 --> 00:12:23.744
Second: a Zero Trust Assessment
test that needs Security

190
00:12:23.744 --> 00:12:27.270
Exposure Management is
skipped in a G C C tenant.

191
00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:28.482
How is it recorded?

192
00:12:28.482 --> 00:12:30.584
The answer is b: as not applicable,

193
00:12:30.584 --> 00:12:35.408
because Learn says Exposure Management
is available in Public Cloud only.

194
00:12:35.408 --> 00:12:39.382
A feature G C C doesn't offer
goes in the decision record and

195
00:12:39.382 --> 00:12:43.771
the closure register as a disposition,
never as a failed control.

196
00:12:43.771 --> 00:12:48.940
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

197
00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:53.515
To sum up.

198
00:12:53.515 --> 00:12:58.133
You placed each setting in its control
plane: Intune for endpoint policy,

199
00:12:58.133 --> 00:13:02.827
the Defender portal for what Intune
doesn't hold, and one plane per setting.

200
00:13:02.827 --> 00:13:06.865
You checked what G C C offers,
and how to record what it doesn't.

201
00:13:06.865 --> 00:13:11.003
You read incidents through Microsoft
Graph, in the tenant's own cloud.

202
00:13:11.003 --> 00:13:14.615
And you proved the sensor, on
one device and across the fleet.

203
00:13:14.615 --> 00:13:17.424
The evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module:

204
00:13:17.424 --> 00:13:20.389
the role export and the
pim assignment report,

205
00:13:20.389 --> 00:13:24.758
the E D R policy export and its
assignments, the weekly coverage file,

206
00:13:24.758 --> 00:13:27.567
the detection test with
the alert it raised,

207
00:13:27.567 --> 00:13:31.390
and the exception register for
every device not onboarded.

208
00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:35.472
Next is Module 2, prevention
and Attack Surface Reduction,

209
00:13:35.472 --> 00:13:40.140
where the settings move through
audit, warn and block, ring by ring.
